description:
Found during a post-landing audit of the cfg-gated tests in this crate, prompted by bd-ab07b8: the smell there was a cross-platform contract hidden behind `#[cfg(windows)]` so Unix CI never ran it. The inverse is also present and sharper — Windows-only behaviour with NO test at all, on a repo whose Windows CI job does run the suite, so a test WOULD execute there.

Uncovered today (`src/lib.rs`, `install_binary`, landed d3d2929):

    #[cfg(windows)]
    if replaced_existing {
        let backup = windows_sidecar_path(destination);
        fs::rename(destination, &backup).with_context(...)?;
        return match tmp.persist(destination) {
            Ok(_) => { let _ = fs::remove_file(&backup); Ok(replaced_existing) }
            Err(err) => {
                let _ = fs::rename(&backup, destination);
                Err(anyhow!("persist {} failed: {err}", destination.display()))
            }
        };
    }

Neither this branch nor `windows_sidecar_path` is referenced by any test. `grep -n windows_sidecar_path src/lib.rs` returns only the definition and its single call site.

The design is right — a running image cannot be deleted (so cannot be replaced in place) but CAN be renamed, which is exactly why the move-aside exists. The gap is that the RECOVERY half is the part most likely to be wrong and is the part never exercised.

A concrete defect visible in that recovery path, independent of the missing tests:

The restore is best-effort and its result is DISCARDED (`let _ = fs::rename(...)`), and the error returned does not mention the sidecar. So if the restore also fails, the user is told only "persist <dest> failed", while their working installed binary is now sitting at `<tool>.exe.old-<pid>-<nanos>` and `<tool>.exe` does not exist. That is the one outcome in this function where a human must intervene, and it is precisely the outcome that tells them nothing about what to do. `install_binary` can currently uninstall a tool and not say where it went.

WANTED

1. Report the unrecovered case honestly. When the restore rename also fails, say so and name the sidecar path, e.g. "persist <dest> failed: <err>; the previous binary could NOT be restored and is at <backup> — rename it back to <dest> manually". Distinguish it from the recovered case, where the incumbent is intact and a retry is safe.
2. Cover the branch with `#[cfg(windows)]` tests that the Windows CI job will actually run:
   - replacing an existing destination succeeds, returns replaced_existing = true, and leaves NO `.old-*` sidecar behind;
   - a failing persist restores the incumbent byte-for-byte and leaves no sidecar (inject the failure by making the destination directory or the temp persist fail — e.g. hold the destination open, or point at a path the persist cannot satisfy);
   - `windows_sidecar_path` produces a path in the same directory as the destination and does not collide across calls.
3. While there: consider whether the success path should also report the sidecar cleanup failing (`let _ = fs::remove_file(&backup)`), which currently leaves a stale `.old-*` file next to the installed binary forever. Low severity, but it accumulates one file per update.

Note for whoever takes this: it cannot be validated locally on a Linux node. The Windows CI job is the validation boundary, which is exactly why the tests are worth adding rather than reasoning about the branch by eye.
